# A <tool_call> tag a reply mentions is text, not the start of a call. Every place the CUDA server finds GLM calls
# began a block at any "<tool_call>" and ran it to the next "</tool_call>": ThinkSplit's call run (0036/0055), the end
# parser (parse_tool_calls), the content hider and GlmCallStreamer. A reply that quoted the tag ("the literal
# `<tool_call>` markup") before its real call made one unparseable block from the mention to the real call's close:
# in the think block across </think> (the reasoning cut at the mention), in the answer across the prose. The call came
# back as content text, finish_reason "stop", no tool_calls. Live on two Sparks, 30 seeded replies to five prompts that
# make the model talk about the tag, each ending in a real bash call, v1.10's image against the same image with only
# this file changed (every reply token-identical): tool_calls 15/30 -> 30/30, whole and streamed. A streamed mention
# with no </think> yet could also be held and, past THINK_CALL_HOLD, committed as a call.
# Now an opener starts a block only when what follows can be a call's head (_call_head: GLM's name then <arg_key> or
# </tool_call>, Qwen's <function=, or JSON; or the start of one while streaming), so a mention is text and a call's
# value may hold the tag; and a call block in the think block does not cross </think>. A call without arguments is
# <tool_call>name</tool_call> as the chat template writes it, so a word the tags enclose with space ("The tags are
# <tool_call> and </tool_call>.") is text too, whole and streamed (the content hider used to drop it from the stream
# for good). Calls the reply ends on inside the think block (the D5 rule), whole calls, and calls to unoffered tools
# (still text, #103) are as before.
# CPU check: tools/test_think_tag_mention.py.
